INT. FUNERAL HOME - BASEMENT KITCHEN - NIGHT

A low-ceilinged room with a stainless-steel prep table, an old gas range, and a long counter heaped with covered casserole dishes. A hand-lettered sign over the sink reads "PLEASE LABEL YOUR PAN." Upstairs, muffled footsteps and the low murmur of mourners.

DOROTHEA PELLEGRINO (60s, black dress, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain) stands at the counter peeling foil from a lasagna. Her hands are quick, efficient, furious. STANLEY PELLEGRINO (60s, her brother, stiff suit, thinning hair) sits at the prep table with a cup of untouched coffee. ROSALIND VANCOUVER-ADEYEMI (40s, their cousin, heels in one hand) pours wine into a plastic cup.

DOROTHEA
Stanley, bring me the serving spoons.

STANLEY
Where are they?

DOROTHEA
Where they have always been.

STANLEY
I haven't been down here in ten years.

DOROTHEA
Then you won't know where they are. Rosalind, the spoons.

ROSALIND
(over her shoulder)
Drawer by the sink.

Rosalind opens the drawer, fishes out three spoons, and hands them to Dorothea without looking.

DOROTHEA
Thank you.

ROSALIND
Of course.

Dorothea lays the spoons across the dishes. Stanley rises, picks up the coffee, sets it down, picks up a casserole instead.

STANLEY
That's heavy.

DOROTHEA
It is a lasagna.

STANLEY
It's heavy.

DOROTHEA
Then put it down.

STANLEY
I'm trying to help.

Dorothea turns. Her jaw works.

DOROTHEA
You could have helped with the hospital bills.

STANLEY
Dot.

DOROTHEA
You could have helped when Mama was sick.

STANLEY
That's not fair.

ROSALIND
(to the wine)
Here we go.

Stanley slams the casserole on the counter. The foil crumples. Rosalind steps between them, arms out, wine sloshing.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
Everyone! Everyone, upstairs can hear you!

Both siblings freeze. A chair scrapes overhead. Footsteps stop.

DOROTHEA
(low)
Mama would be mortified.

STANLEY
She'd be laughing.

DOROTHEA
She would not.

STANLEY
She would. She loved a good fight.

Dorothea almost smiles. She hides it behind a cough. Rosalind sets down her cup and takes both of their hands.

ROSALIND
Listen to me. We are going to carry these dishes upstairs, and we are going to be kind to each other.

STANLEY
Rosalind.

ROSALIND
And afterward, you can scream at me in the parking lot.

DOROTHEA
(muttering)
I will.

STANLEY
(muttering)
I might.

Rosalind lets go. The three of them stare at one another, then at the counter full of lasagna and pie and ham.

Stanley lifts a tray. Dorothea lifts another. Rosalind hooks her shoes on two fingers and grabs the wine.

They file up the stairs one behind the other, the dishes clinking.
